<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">ʻUmi-a-Līloa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1500</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Umi-a-Liloa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">ʻUmi-a-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liloa">Līloa</a> was ruler (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alii">aliʻi</a> nui) of the island of Hawaiʻi around 1500, a son of Līloa by a woman of lower rank. After Līloa's death he overthrew his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakau">Hākau</a> and united the island's six districts under a single ruler. Tradition credits him with building temples, organising land divisions and farming, and ruling from Waipiʻo and later from the Kona coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/umi-a-liloa.jpg" alt="ʻUmi-a-Līloa" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hiart · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lei_Niho_Palaoa_(Neck_Ornament),_19th_century,_Carved_sperm_whale_tooth,_braided_human_hair,_olona_cordage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to the saga, his mother Akahiakuleana, known as Akahi, met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liloa">Līloa</a> when he was visiting Hamakua; each was married to someone else. Līloa told her that if she bore a son she should send him with the royal tokens he gave her as proof. When ʻUmi was 15 or 16 and his stepfather was punishing him, Akahi revealed the hidden tokens, the royal malo and lei niho palaoa worn only by high chiefs, and sent him to Waipiʻo.</p><p>ʻUmi walked into the sacred enclosure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liloa">Līloa</a>'s palace, where trespass was punishable by death, woke the king and laid the tokens at his feet, and Līloa acknowledged him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakau">Hākau</a>, Līloa's first-born son by his first wife Pinea, was angered; Līloa assured him he would be king and his brother would serve him. ʻUmi was raised at court on an equal footing and found favour with his father, which deepened Hākau's dislike.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakau">Hākau</a> inherited the kingdom and in time became brutal, and ʻUmi withdrew to another district, where he took wives, gathered followers and became known for feeding the people. Hākau insulted his father's two favourite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahuna">kahuna</a>, Nunu and Ka-hohe of the priestly class of Lono, by refusing them food when they were ill, and they joined ʻUmi's revolt through Kaoleioku, high priest of the temple of Manini. While Hākau's warriors were away gathering feathers for their war regalia, ʻUmi's men came with bundles presented as offerings, filled in fact with stones, and stoned the undefended king to death.</p><p>The other chiefs then claimed their districts for themselves. On the priests' advice ʻUmi married women of high rank, among them his half-sister Kapukini and a daughter of the ruler of Hilo, who had sheltered him, and in the end conquered the whole island. He let his three most faithful companions and the two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahuna">kahuna</a> help govern it. His wife Piʻikea, daughter of Piʻilani of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a>, bore him a son, Kumalae, and a daughter, Aihākōkō.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>ʻUmi was remembered as a just and religious ruler, and his story is one of the most popular hero sagas of Hawaiian history. Historians regard it as probably embellished, as such sagas are, while holding that a portion of it is historically accurate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “ʻUmi-a-Līloa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1500 <b>ʻUmi-a-Līloa</b>, son of the ruler Līloa, overthrew his half-brother Hākau and united the island's six districts under one aliʻi nui.</blockquote><a href="/state/chiefdom-of-hawaii-island">Chiefdom of Hawaii Island</a></li></ul></section>
